WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:06.384
Ian stood at the window.

2
00:00:06.385 --> 00:00:12.929
Right outside a noisy robin fluttered about, but Ian scarcely noticed it.

3
00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:17.515
His eyes were focused instead on the backyard garden.

4
00:00:17.516 --> 00:00:26.763
Normally by Father's Day, strawberries that Dad had planted were ripening, and green beans were coiling up their poles.

5
00:00:26.764 --> 00:00:32.140
But this year the garden was just an ugly patch of dirt and weeds.

6
00:00:32.141 --> 00:00:35.416
Ian and Mom hadn't planted a thing.

7
00:00:35.417 --> 00:00:41.373
Ian closed his eyes, remembering last year's Father's Day celebration.

8
00:00:41.374 --> 00:00:47.495
They'd gone to a baseball game, and later Dad had grilled hamburgers.

9
00:00:47.496 --> 00:00:52.243
"Today is definitely going to be different," Ian thought glumly.

10
00:00:52.244 --> 00:00:57.695
It was the first Father's Day since Dad had died. And—

11
00:00:57.696 --> 00:01:02.626
"Ian! Breakfast!" Mom called.

12
00:01:02.627 --> 00:01:07.346
Downstairs, Ian poured syrup on his pancakes.

13
00:01:07.347 --> 00:01:11.477
"Are we doing anything today?" he asked blankly.

14
00:01:11.478 --> 00:01:16.439
Mom looked up from her plate, quickly brushing away a few tears.

15
00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:18.778
"Yes," she said briskly.

16
00:01:18.779 --> 00:01:22.189
"We're going to Meadow Bird Sanctuary.

17
00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:26.372
It's a beautiful day, and there's a nice trail around the pond.

18
00:01:26.373 --> 00:01:29.716
We'll see lots of birds." She paused.

19
00:01:29.717 --> 00:01:33.011
"Dad loved watching birds."

20
00:01:33.012 --> 00:01:38.469
Ian sighed. "I remember."

21
00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:40.620
"What's a sanctuary anyway?"

22
00:01:40.621 --> 00:01:44.047
Ian asked as Mom parked in a gravel lot.

23
00:01:44.048 --> 00:01:48.876
"I guess you could call it a place of refuge," Mom said.

24
00:01:48.877 --> 00:01:55.080
"It's land preserved for plants and wildlife, to keep them safe and help them thrive."

25
00:01:55.081 --> 00:02:00.889
Ian saw some dense woods with trails forking off in different directions.

26
00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:08.449
A few people stood near a Welcome sign—a mother and a little girl and an older man with binoculars.

27
00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:13.983
"Looks like a tour's about to start," Mom said. "Good."

28
00:02:13.984 --> 00:02:19.404
A guide introduced herself as Beth, and the group set off.

29
00:02:19.405 --> 00:02:25.390
As they wandered through the woods, Beth pointed out various plants and trees.

30
00:02:25.391 --> 00:02:32.601
Ian couldn't keep track of any of the plants' names—except for the poison ivy creeping along the trail.

31
00:02:32.602 --> 00:02:37.170
Suddenly Beth halted and pointed upward. "Look."

32
00:02:37.171 --> 00:02:41.604
Ian gazed up at the branches of a tall tree.

33
00:02:41.605 --> 00:02:45.042
He saw a brown bird near a nest.

34
00:02:45.043 --> 00:02:48.983
"A mommy bird!" the little girl cried.

35
00:02:48.984 --> 00:02:52.483
"She's feeding her babies!"

36
00:02:52.484 --> 00:02:56.339
"Actually, that's a daddy bird," Beth said.

37
00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:04.222
"And since today is Father's Day, it's the perfect time to tell you that many birds make great fathers!"

38
00:03:04.223 --> 00:03:07.896
The man with the binoculars nodded eagerly.

39
00:03:07.897 --> 00:03:13.566
"Birds actually make much better fathers than mammals do."

40
00:03:13.567 --> 00:03:17.378
"That's true, Mr. Avery," Beth said.

41
00:03:17.379 --> 00:03:24.481
"While male mammals rarely feed their young, some male birds bring food to their babies.

42
00:03:24.482 --> 00:03:30.764
Plus, village weavers build nests, and emperor penguins keep eggs warm.

43
00:03:30.765 --> 00:03:37.458
Did you know that some male songbirds teach their sons how to sing?"

44
00:03:37.459 --> 00:03:40.550
"Yes!" Mr. Avery burst out.

45
00:03:40.551 --> 00:03:44.078
"The white-throated sparrow does that!"

46
00:03:44.079 --> 00:03:46.909
Ian and Mom exchanged grins.

47
00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:51.857
Mr. Avery was certainly very enthusiastic about birds.

48
00:03:51.858 --> 00:03:55.713
"Dad would love this guy!" Ian thought.

49
00:03:55.714 --> 00:04:00.501
As they circled the pond, Beth showed them some wading birds.

50
00:04:00.502 --> 00:04:04.013
Ian stopped to watch a great blue heron.

51
00:04:04.014 --> 00:04:10.434
The tall bird stood silently in shallow water, waiting for fish to swim by.

52
00:04:10.435 --> 00:04:17.009
Its legs were so skinny and long, they made Ian think of brittle sticks.

53
00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:21.679
Mr. Avery was watching too.

54
00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:27.454
"Bird-watching requires stillness and patience," he said quietly.

55
00:04:27.455 --> 00:04:32.281
"But it always brings me delight—and peace."

56
00:04:32.282 --> 00:04:37.627
After the tour Ian and Mom bought some lunch and sat outside on a bench.

57
00:04:37.628 --> 00:04:44.461
The sun felt warm, and the air inside the sanctuary rang with birds' clear voices.

58
00:04:44.462 --> 00:04:48.186
"This is Dad's kind of place," Ian said.

59
00:04:48.187 --> 00:04:52.495
Mom smiled. "It sure is."

60
00:04:52.496 --> 00:04:57.567
"I know what we should do when we get home," Ian said enthusiastically.

61
00:04:57.568 --> 00:05:01.928
Mom looked at him. "What?"

62
00:05:01.929 --> 00:05:08.150
"Plant some vegetables! Dad would have liked that too."

